       "AIMessage(content='Parrots “talk” because they’re incredibly good mimics and very social animals. Here’s what’s going on:\\n\\n| Why they mimic | What it means |\\n|----------------|---------------|\\n| **Syrinx anatomy** | Parrots have a highly flexible vocal organ (the syrinx) that lets them produce a wide range of sounds, from chirps to human speech. |\\n| **Social learning** | In the wild, parrots learn calls, warnings, and mating signals from their flock. Mimicking helps them fit in and bond with others. |\\n| **Attention‑seeking** | In a human household, parrots discover that repeating words or sounds gets a reaction—food, petting, or a laugh—so they keep doing it. |\\n| **Cognitive ability** | Many species (e.g., African grey, budgerigar, cockatoo) have excellent memory and can associate words with meanings, even using them contextually. |\\n\\n**Short answer:** Parrots mimic because their physiology allows it, their brains are wired for social learning, and they use it to communicate and get attention. They don’t “talk” in the human sense of forming sentences, but they can learn and use words in a way that feels conversational.', additional_kwargs={'reasoning_content': 'The user asks: \"hello, why do parrots talk?\" We need to answer. Provide explanation: parrots mimic sounds, have vocal apparatus, social behavior, intelligence, etc. Should be friendly, concise. Also maybe mention that parrots mimic because they have a syrinx, social learning, etc. Also mention that parrots are highly intelligent and use vocalizations for communication. They learn by listening to humans and other parrots. They have the ability to mimic because of their vocal organs, etc. Also mention that parrots are not actually \"talking\" in the sense of forming language, but they mimic. Provide details: parrots have a unique syrinx, they can produce a wide range of sounds, they are social, they use mimicry to bond, they can learn words and use them appropriately. Also mention that parrots are capable of learning simple words, but they don\\'t have full understanding of language. They mimic because they are social and mimic to fit into group. They also do it for attention. Provide some fun facts. Should be friendly. Let\\'s produce a concise answer.'}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 485, 'prompt_tokens': 79, 'total_tokens': 564, 'completion_time': 0.541113164, 'completion_tokens_details': {'reasoning_tokens': 222}, 'prompt_time': 0.003946988, 'prompt_tokens_details': None, 'queue_time': 0.304012946, 'total_time': 0.545060152}, 'model_name': 'openai/gpt-oss-20b', 'system_fingerprint': 'fp_a4315eb300', 'service_tier': 'on_demand', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_provider': 'groq'}, id='lc_run--01a08785-52d9-78e3-b3af-302d95218a99-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 79, 'output_tokens': 485, 'total_tokens': 564, 'output_token_details': {'reasoning': 222}})"
